Next, we consider the token-selective KD proposed in the paper. We consider "soft selection" based on entropy and sigmoid gating function as in Eq. (10) in the paper. The distillation objective has the following form (only forward KL is used):

$$
L_{\text{TS}} = \sigma(H_i(q)-H_i(p)) \cdot \mathcal D_{KL}(p\|q)
$$

where $H_i(p)$ is the entropy of token $i$ evaluated by $p$, and the sigmoid function $\sigma(\cdot)$ enables the "soft selection" based on the entropy difference. Its gradient is given by

$$
\frac{dL_{\text{TS}}}{dz_i}
= \sigma(H_i(q)-H_i(p))[p_i - q_i] \tag{2}
$$

Note that the $\sigma(\cdot)$ term is simply a weight and is computed with no gradient flow to $p$ and $q$ in $\sigma(\cdot)$ (we apply stop-gradient). We can also define the rescaling factor with respect to student models $q$ and $q'$ ($q$ is the weaker student):

$$
\nabla_i^\text{TS} := 
\frac{\sigma(H_i(q)-H_i(p))(p_i-q_i)}
{\sigma(H_i(q')-H_i(p))(p_i-q_i')} \tag{3}
$$

From (3), we have that

$$
\nabla_i^{\textrm{TS}}=\underbrace{
\frac{\sigma\left(H_i(q)-H_i(p)\right)}
{\sigma\left(H_i(q')-H_i(p)\right)}
}_{C}
\nabla_i
$$


Thus, the token selection has a higher rescaling factor than the conventional KD if \(C>1\). We have that 

$$
C =\frac{\sigma(H_i(q)-H_i(p))}
{\sigma(H_i(q')-H_i(p))}
=\frac{\exp(-H_i(p)) + \exp(-H_i(q'))}
{\exp(-H_i(p)) + \exp(-H_i(q))} \tag{4}
$$


If the weaker student $q$ is *less* confident than $q'$ in token $i$, it has *higher* uncertainty. Thus, $H_i(q) > H_i(q')$, which implies that $C>1$. Conversely, if the confidence of the student is higher, $C<1$. Thus, the student will perceive $C>1$ during the early stage of training, while it will perceive $C<1$ during the later stage. This leads to two effects on the student models at different stages of training.

**(1) Reinforced gradient.** In the early stage of training, the student model is relatively weak and perceives $C$ as $C>1$. This means that the scaling factor under token selection is stronger than the conventional KD. Thus, token selection can **reinforce** the gradient towards the "best" token. Such a reinforced gradient helps establish logical abilities in the early stage, facilitating the convergence of training.

**(2) Label smoothing.** In the later stage of the training, the student is well-trained and is likely to have basic reasoning capabilities. The student will perceive $C$ as $C<1$ compared to the earlier version of itself. This means that the gradient is perceived weaker than the conventional KD. Thus, the student can *diversify* its reasoning process based on already established knowledge. Since the gradient to "best" answer has weakened, we can liken the approach to **label smoothing** [5]. It allows more exploration of parameter space for reasoning, which is consistent with our student-centric approach.

**Action:** We will add the analysis to Appendix A.8 (due to space limitation). Please see Appendix A.8 of the revised paper.

**References**

[1] Plackett, Robin L. "The analysis of permutations." Journal of the Royal Statistical Society Series C: Applied Statistics 24.2 (1975): 193-202.

[2] Xia, Fen, et al. "Listwise approach to learning to rank: theory and algorithm." Proceedings of the 25th international conference on Machine learning. 2008.

[3] Tang, Jiaxi, et al. "Understanding and improving knowledge distillation." arXiv preprint arXiv:2002.03532 (2020).

[4] Yoshua Bengio, Jérôme Louradour, Ronan Collobert, and Jason Weston. Curriculum learning. In
Proceedings of the 26th annual international conference on machine learning, pp. 41–48. ACM,
2009.

[5] Müller, Rafael, Simon Kornblith, and Geoffrey E. Hinton. "When does label smoothing help?." Advances in neural information processing systems 32 (2019).
